    <td
      class="d-block color-fg-default comment-body markdown-body js-comment-body"
      
    >
        <p dir="auto">One limitation is that the current implementation doesn't address how to guard calls to external functions using tracked variables—unless those functions are manually replaced with wrapper functions. This approach, however, causes the executable code to diverge significantly from the original unverified version.</p>
<p dir="auto">A potential solution to use a procedural macro that recognizes when certain external functions are being guarded by wrapper functions. The mapping between external functions and their verified counterparts could be hard-coded (for standard library functions) or loaded from a configuration file.</p>
<p dir="auto">For example, a call to core::ptr::read(src: *const T) -&gt; T could be rewritten as vstd::ptr_mut_read(src: *const T, Tracked(perm): Tracked&lt;&amp;mut PointsTo&gt;) -&gt; T in verification mode. The macro would replace core::ptr::read with vstd::ptr_mut_read and insert a corresponding proof_with!(Tracked(perm)) annotation automatically. While this mechanism is theoretically workable, it may be too hacky.</p>
<div class="highlight highlight-source-rust notranslate position-relative overflow-auto" dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="#[verus_spec(
   with Tracked(perm): Tracked(PointsTo&lt;u64&gt;),
  requires ...
)
]
unsafe fn test_ptr_read(src: *const u64) -&gt; u64 {
  proof_with!(Tracked(perm));
  let val = core::ptr::read(src);
  val
}
"><pre class="notranslate"><span class="pl-c1">#<span class="pl-kos">[</span>verus_spec<span class="pl-kos">(</span></span>
<span class="pl-c1">   with <span class="pl-v">Tracked</span><span class="pl-kos">(</span>perm<span class="pl-kos">)</span><span class="pl-kos">:</span> <span class="pl-v">Tracked</span><span class="pl-kos">(</span><span class="pl-v">PointsTo</span>&lt;<span class="pl-smi">u64</span>&gt;<span class="pl-kos">)</span><span class="pl-kos">,</span></span>
<span class="pl-c1">  requires ...</span>
<span class="pl-c1"><span class="pl-kos">)</span></span>
<span class="pl-c1"><span class="pl-kos">]</span></span>
<span class="pl-k">unsafe</span> <span class="pl-k">fn</span> <span class="pl-en">test_ptr_read</span><span class="pl-kos">(</span><span class="pl-s1">src</span><span class="pl-kos">:</span> <span class="pl-c1">*</span><span class="pl-k">const</span> <span class="pl-smi">u64</span><span class="pl-kos">)</span> -&gt; <span class="pl-smi">u64</span> <span class="pl-kos">{</span>
  <span class="pl-en">proof_with</span><span class="pl-en">!</span><span class="pl-kos">(</span><span class="pl-v">Tracked</span><span class="pl-kos">(</span>perm<span class="pl-kos">)</span><span class="pl-kos">)</span><span class="pl-kos">;</span>
  <span class="pl-k">let</span> val = core<span class="pl-kos">::</span>ptr<span class="pl-kos">::</span><span class="pl-en">read</span><span class="pl-kos">(</span>src<span class="pl-kos">)</span><span class="pl-kos">;</span>
  val
<span class="pl-kos">}</span></pre></div>
<div class="highlight highlight-source-rust notranslate position-relative overflow-auto" dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="unsafe fn test_ptr_read(src: *const u64) -&gt; u64 
requires ...
{
  let val = vstd::ptr_mut_read(src, Tracked(perm));
  val
}
"><pre class="notranslate"><span class="pl-k">unsafe</span> <span class="pl-k">fn</span> <span class="pl-en">test_ptr_read</span><span class="pl-kos">(</span><span class="pl-s1">src</span><span class="pl-kos">:</span> <span class="pl-c1">*</span><span class="pl-k">const</span> <span class="pl-smi">u64</span><span class="pl-kos">)</span> -&gt; <span class="pl-smi">u64</span><span class="pl-kos"></span> 
requires ...
<span class="pl-kos">{</span>
  <span class="pl-k">let</span> val = vstd<span class="pl-kos">::</span><span class="pl-en">ptr_mut_read</span><span class="pl-kos">(</span>src<span class="pl-kos">,</span> <span class="pl-en">Tracked</span><span class="pl-kos">(</span>perm<span class="pl-kos">)</span><span class="pl-kos">)</span><span class="pl-kos">;</span>
  val
<span class="pl-kos">}</span><span class="pl-kos"></span></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">I think a better solution is to do such replacement by rust_verifier. It should be more easier to understand and flexible if verifier can identify the extra arguments from assume_specification and the extra arguments from proof_with!().</p>
<div class="snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="verus!{
assume_specification [core::ptr::read] (src: *const T,  Tracked(perm): Tracked&lt;&amp;mut PointsTo&lt;T&gt;&gt;) -&gt; (ret: T)
requires ...
ensures ...
}"><pre class="notranslate"><code class="notranslate">verus!{
assume_specification [core::ptr::read] (src: *const T,  Tracked(perm): Tracked&lt;&amp;mut PointsTo&lt;T&gt;&gt;) -&gt; (ret: T)
requires ...
ensures ...
}
</code></pre></div>
    </td>
  </tr>

